WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:07.436
We've established that RAG works to retrieve&nbsp;knowledge from a data source by chunking that&nbsp;data source, embedding the chunks,

00:00:07.436 --> 00:00:12.631
then injecting&nbsp;the results of a similarity search into
the&nbsp;prompt of the LLM.

00:00:12.631 --> 00:00:17.417
We'll now cover the basics&nbsp;of how documents
get split into chunks for&nbsp;retrieval.

00:00:17.417 --> 00:00:27.193
Along the way, we'll also be introducing&nbsp;a high-level library that we'll use for a variety&nbsp;of tasks later on in this course called Langchain.

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:32.408
Document chunking, while simple in concept,
poses some tricky questions.

00:00:32.408 --> 00:00:38.060
First, how long do&nbsp;we make our chunks?
Too short and they may lack&nbsp;context.

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:45.190
Too long and they may contain irrelevant&nbsp;information that distorts both the embedding and&nbsp;the LLM generation.

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:51.168
There are several ways&nbsp;to perform chunking and we encourage 
you to&nbsp;experiment with all of these options.

00:00:51.168 --> 00:00:57.740
First, we have&nbsp;fixed size, which splits documents into segments 
of&nbsp;a predetermined number of sentences.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:07.842
Next, we have&nbsp;dynamic, which sets a minimum and maximum size for&nbsp;chunks and attempts to group them by paragraph&nbsp;headers or other dynamic patterns.

00:01:07.842 --> 00:01:15.812
Finally, we&nbsp;have advanced, which utilizes embedding models&nbsp;to identify the conclusion of a thought and the&nbsp;beginning of a new one.

00:01:15.812 --> 00:01:22.439
Like so much in ML, a lot&nbsp;of this comes down to trial and error 
with your&nbsp;own data and requirements.

00:01:22.439 --> 00:01:28.813
The method that we'll&nbsp;be chunking with in our upcoming exercise is&nbsp;Langchain's recursive character text splitter,

00:01:28.813 --> 00:01:34.650
which attempts paragraph graph level splits but&nbsp;can descend to smaller units
if the chunks don't&nbsp;fit.

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:39.116
The recursive text splitter is a great go-to&nbsp;technique
to have in your pocket.

00:01:39.116 --> 00:01:46.931
Another challenge&nbsp;of document chunking arises when dealing with edge&nbsp;cases, where relevant information is split across&nbsp;multiple chunks.

00:01:46.931 --> 00:01:54.857
Take this, for example. In chunk&nbsp;one we have,
"The Mona Lisa is a famous painting by&nbsp;Leonardo da Vinci.

00:01:54.857 --> 00:02:01.463
"It was painted in the early 16th&nbsp;century
and is now housed in the Louvre Museum&nbsp;in Paris.

00:02:01.463 --> 00:02:09.918
"The painting is known for the enigmatic&nbsp;smile of its subject, a mysterious woman who has&nbsp;intrigued viewers for centuries."

00:02:09.918 --> 00:02:14.560
In chunk two we&nbsp;have, "Many theories have been proposed
about&nbsp;the woman,

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:21.926
"but the most widely accepted theory&nbsp;is that she is Lisa Gherardini, the wife of a wealthy&nbsp;Florentine merchant.

00:02:21.926 --> 00:02:30.107
"The painting's title "Mona Lisa,"&nbsp;is a shortened form of "Madonna Lisa," which means&nbsp;"Lady Lisa" in Italian."

00:02:30.107 --> 00:02:36.000
Imagine a user queried the&nbsp;vector database with,
"Who was the subject of the&nbsp;Mona Lisa?"

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.432
The search would likely return chunk&nbsp;one
and the model might answer back,

00:02:40.432 --> 00:02:45.000
the Mona&nbsp;Lisa is a mysterious woman
who has intrigued&nbsp;viewers for centuries.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:49.546
In doing so, it would miss&nbsp;the valuable information in chunk two.

00:02:49.546 --> 00:02:54.404
To address&nbsp;this, we can overlap the first and last
sections of&nbsp;each chunk.

00:02:54.404 --> 00:03:04.686
While not a perfect fix, this can help&nbsp;cut down on a loss of context during retrieval. The&nbsp;changes to the document are highlighted in yellow&nbsp;on this slide.

00:03:04.686 --> 00:03:11.440
The overlap chunk one now shows, "The&nbsp;
"Mona Lisa" is a famous painting by Leonardo da Vinci.

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:18.000
"It was painted in the early 16th century and is&nbsp;
now housed in the Louvre museum in Paris.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:25.260
"The painting&nbsp;is known for the enigmatic smile of its subject,&nbsp;
a mysterious woman who has intrigued viewers for&nbsp;centuries.

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:35.148
"Many theories have been proposed about&nbsp;the woman but the most widely accepted theory&nbsp;is that she is Lisa Gherardini the wife of&nbsp;a wealthy Florentine merchant."

00:03:35.148 --> 00:03:40.761
In chunk two, we have, "The painting is known for the enigmatic&nbsp;
smile of its subject,

00:03:40.761 --> 00:03:44.252
"a mysterious woman who has&nbsp;intrigued viewers for centuries.

00:03:44.252 --> 00:03:51.000
"Many theories&nbsp;have been proposed about the woman but the&nbsp;most widely accepted theory is that she is Lisa&nbsp;Gheradini,

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.800
"the wife of a wealthy Florentine Merchant.

00:03:53.800 --> 00:04:02.822
"The painting's title "Mona Lisa," is a shortened form&nbsp;
of "Madonna Lisa," which means "Lady Lisa" in Italian."

00:04:02.822 --> 00:04:09.922
With this overlap, the most likely identity of the&nbsp;
Mona Lisa is contained within the overlapped chunk&nbsp;one.

00:04:09.922 --> 00:04:18.000
As a result, our model can draw upon its&nbsp;information in its response and offer a more&nbsp;helpful answer to the users.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:24.694
Beyond chunking itself,&nbsp;we need to consider how chunks are embedded
and&nbsp;stored in the vector database.

00:04:24.694 --> 00:04:32.336
Let's briefly touch&nbsp;on some of these. Consider incorporating various&nbsp;forms of metadata into your chunks.

00:04:32.336 --> 00:04:37.191
The data&nbsp;will be fed to the LLM and rendered to the
user in&nbsp;various ways.

00:04:37.191 --> 00:04:45.602
For instance, if you hope to cite the&nbsp;source in your output, include a URL so users can&nbsp;access the source material directly.

00:04:45.602 --> 00:04:50.788
Additionally,&nbsp;consider page numbers
or other ways to cite the&nbsp;source.

00:04:50.788 --> 00:04:58.661
Save the chunks index so it's placed in the&nbsp;document can be retrieved, this is also useful if&nbsp;you want to return the chunks immediately

00:04:58.661 --> 00:05:03.480
before&nbsp;and after the retrieved chunk to grant more context.

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:07.198
Save any metadata you'd like to filter on in the&nbsp;future.

00:05:07.198 --> 00:05:16.040
In practice, you'll often want to reduce&nbsp;the scope of a query down to a subset of the&nbsp;database. You may want to version the chunking.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:23.676
If you try different chunking strategies or sizes&nbsp;in the future, you can identify the technique used&nbsp;and or the date.

00:05:23.676 --> 00:05:27.680
If you have long chunks of&nbsp;text and want to quickly identify them,

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:33.360
you could also pass the chunk to a language model&nbsp;
and obtain a short summary of the chunk instead.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:39.687
In fact, there are plenty of other interesting&nbsp;applications of LLMs 
during chunking uploads&nbsp;available, as well,

00:05:39.687 --> 00:05:46.314
such as extracting keywords&nbsp;or generating hypothetical questions 
that could&nbsp;be posed about the chunk.

00:05:46.314 --> 00:05:50.760
We'll dive into some of&nbsp;these advanced techniques
later on in the lesson.

